y, and on move 18 the 14k's D1 handed Opus 5.5 a +22.1 lead. Opus 5.5 gave it back over the next two moves (15.3 points at move 19 and 14.5 at move 21), and from -7.7 it never got ahead again. The lead sat near -15.5 for the last twenty moves before the resignation.", "moments": [{"turn": 10, "text": "The 14k's F3 lost 6.8 points and turned -4.4 into +2.4 for Black. The engine preferred G4, which Opus 5.5 then played at move 11. It was the first sign that this game would be a trade of mistakes."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 14k played D1 on the first line, next to Opus 5.5's D2 stone, and lost 15.3 points. Black's lead jumped from +6.8 to +22.1, the biggest edge of the game."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with F1 and gave back the full 15.3 points, so the lead fell from +22.1 to +6.8. The engine's first choice was G6, in the centre where the White stones were, not on the bottom edge."}, {"turn": 21, "text": "E1 lost another 14.5 points and turned +6.7 into -7.7. The engine wanted C6 here. After this Opus 5.5 trailed for the rest of the game."}, {"turn": 51, "text": "B5 lost 5.6 points and pushed the score from -7.6 to -13.2, even though the engine's pick, A5, was in the same corner. The 14k took A5 next. After this the lead drifted to -15.5 and the last two dozen moves only filled in the borders."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties with a danger flag, and count.py only flood-fills empty regions to tally territory with player-named dead stones removed. Both work on the current position, and neither ranks moves, loops over candidates, or looks ahead.", "stop": false}}